WEBVTT

00:00:00.102 --> 00:00:03.362
A very common beginner mistake is trying to compare
a value to

00:00:03.388 --> 00:00:08.939
null using the equals operator For example writing
email address

00:00:08.965 --> 00:00:13.245
equals null does not test whether the email is
missing The result

00:00:13.271 --> 00:00:18.593
is UNKNOWN The same problem happens with not equal
to null To

00:00:18.619 --> 00:00:23.116
test for missing data SQL provides special
predicates IS NULL and IS

00:00:23.129 --> 00:00:28.696
NOT NULL The code on this slide intentionally
demonstrates the incorrect

00:00:28.722 --> 00:00:33.285
comparison so you can see what PostgreSQL returns
Run it in the

00:00:33.311 --> 00:00:38.169
browser and look at the equals-null and
not-equals-null columns You can also

00:00:38.195 --> 00:00:43.811
copy the code into pgAdmin The rule to remember is
simple when

00:00:43.836 --> 00:00:47.174
checking whether a value is missing use IS NULL or
IS NOT

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:51.724
NULL Do not use ordinary equals or not-equals
comparisons
